{"decision": "I now fully understand the bug. `iter_slice` converts start/stop to physical storage offsets via `_get_real_index`, then applies `islice` over the visible-item iteration. After removals, physical offsets exceed visible positions, so slices return wrong contents. The fix: slice by visible positions only — normalize start/stop against visible length (`len(self)`), handle negative step by reversing the stream and taking abs(step) per the documented Boltons convention, then `islice` over the visible stream. Scalar indexing (`__getitem__` int path) correctly keeps using `_get_real_index` and is unchanged.", "actions": [{"tool": "edit", "path": "boltons/setutils.py", "edits": [{"old": "    def iter_slice(self, start, stop, step=None):
        "iterate over a slice of the set"
        iterable = self
        if start is not None:
            start = self._get_real_index(start)
        if stop is not None:
            stop = self._get_real_index(stop)
        if step is not None and step < 0:
            step = -step
            iterable = reversed(self)
        return islice(iterable, start, stop, step)", "new": "    def iter_slice(self, start, stop, step=None):
        "iterate over a slice of the set"
        iterable = self
        if step is not None and step < 0:
            step = -step
            iterable = reversed(self)
        n = len(self)
        if start is not None:
            if start < 0:
                start += n
            if start < 0:
                start = 0
            elif start > n:
                start = n
        if stop is not None:
            if stop < 0:
                stop += n
            if stop < 0:
                stop = 0
            elif stop > n:
                stop = n
        return islice(iterable, start, stop, step)"}]}]}
